  <div class="content-callout">
    <h2>The Short Answer</h2>
    <p>
      Both sell pay-per-GB data with no single flagship unlimited plan, so this isn't an unlimited-vs-metered call like <a href="/compare/holafly-vs-airalo/">Holafly vs Airalo</a>. It's a question of how precisely you want to buy. <strong>Airalo</strong> sells a handful of preset bundle sizes per country and is the more recognized name with the bigger catalog. <strong>eSIM Card</strong> sells far more size-and-length combinations per country, plus an add-on virtual phone number, for travelers who know exactly what they need.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>The Core Difference: Preset Bundles vs. Granular Sizing</h2>
  <p>
    Airalo's model is simple: pick a country or region, pick one of a few preset data sizes (say 1GB, 3GB, 5GB), pick a validity window, done. It also sells <strong>unlimited regional eSIMs</strong> (Europe, Asia, North America, Latin America, the Caribbean, MENA, Africa, Australia) that run at full speed for the first 3GB each day before throttling to 1Mbps until midnight, per Airalo's own fair-use policy.
  </p>
  <p>
    eSIM Card goes narrower on purpose. Its United States page alone lists roughly 30 combinations: seven fixed-data tiers (1GB through 100GB) each sold across several validity windows from 3 to 90 days, plus two unlimited tiers ("Unlimited" and "Unlimited Plus") running 1 to 30 days. eSIM Card's unlimited plans also throttle after a daily full-speed allowance, but unlike Airalo, it doesn't publish the actual GB number, just "an allowed limit of data at full speed per 24 hours."
  </p>

