    </ul>
    <p style="margin-top:.75rem;margin-bottom:0;color:#63553F;">Neither code is ours: Airalo's is a $3 Airmoney referral code (ours is <a href="/airalo/">ADAMLINDS3</a>, listed on the Airalo page), and eSIM Card's ESIM10 is a public code advertised directly on its own homepage. Neither provider runs the kind of affiliate program that supports a code like <a href="/holafly/">Holafly</a>'s or <a href="/ubigi/">Ubigi</a>'s.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Is Airalo or eSIM Card the Right Pick</h2>
  <div class="decision-grid">
    <div class="decision-card decision-card--primary">
      <h3>Go with Airalo if&hellip;</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>You want the bigger, more recognized catalog and app ecosystem</li>
        <li>A multi-country trip where a regional unlimited eSIM covers several stops at once</li>
        <li>You're fine rounding up to the nearest preset bundle size</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="decision-card decision-card--secondary">
      <h3>Go with eSIM Card if&hellip;</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>You know your exact GB and day count and want a plan sized to match instead of rounding up</li>
        <li>You want the option to add a virtual phone number from the same app, for a separate fee</li>
        <li>You're confident in the plan before buying, since eSIM Card's refund policy is strict once it's installed or used</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Two Things Worth Knowing Before You Decide</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Airalo's "many different codes" aren't a messy affiliate program, they're individual referral links.</strong> Airalo's Airmoney program gives every user their own $3-off code, which is why dozens of different codes circulate for it instead of one official promo. eSIM Card has exactly one public code, ESIM10, advertised on its own homepage.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Only Airalo tells you the daily unlimited-speed number.</strong> Airalo's regional unlimited plans run full speed for 3GB a day, then throttle to 1Mbps. eSIM Card's unlimited tiers also throttle daily but don't publish the GB figure, so you're trusting an unnamed limit rather than a stated one.
  </p>
  <p>
    For device setup and eSIM basics that apply to both providers, see our <a href="/compatible-devices/">eSIM compatible devices</a> guide. We've also written a more in-depth <a href="https://www.adamandlinds.com/blog/best-prepaid-sim-cards-tourists/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">field-tested comparison of Holafly, Airalo, and Airhub</a> on our travel site.
  </p>

  <!-- FAQ -->
  <h2>Airalo vs eSIM Card FAQ</h2>
  <div class="content-faq">
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Which is cheaper, Airalo or eSIM Card</h3>
      <p>It depends on the exact plan size you match them on. eSIM Card's smallest US tier (1GB, 3 days at $1.92) undercuts Airalo's smallest US tier (1GB at $4.50), but eSIM Card also sells far more size-and-length combinations, so a true comparison means matching both the GB and the number of days, not just picking the cheapest-looking number.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Does either have a real unlimited plan with no cap at all</h3>
      <p>No, both throttle after heavy same-day use. Airalo publishes the exact number for its regional unlimited plans (3GB/day full speed, then 1Mbps). eSIM Card's unlimited tiers throttle too, but it doesn't publish the daily GB figure, only "an allowed limit of data at full speed per 24 hours."</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Can I get a phone number with either one</h3>
      <p>eSIM Card sells a separate virtual phone number add-on starting around $2.40 inside its own app. Airalo has no equivalent phone-number product.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Which is easier to get a refund from</h3>
      <p>Neither is generous once a plan is installed or used. eSIM Card's own policy limits refunds to narrow cases like a genuine coverage failure or technical fault with zero data consumed, claimed within 30 days. Buy the plan you're confident about on either platform.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

